  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Type 2 diabetes is diagnosed using specific blood tests that measure the amount of glucose (sugar) in your blood.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "using specific blood tests that measure the amount of glucose (sugar) in your blood"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Healthcare providers follow guidelines from organizations like the American Diabetes Association to confirm a diagnosis.",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "American Diabetes Association",
            "link": "https://diabetes.org/about-diabetes/diagnosis"
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Diagnostic Blood Tests"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "A1C Test: Measures your average blood sugar level over the past three months. No fasting is required. A result of 6.5% or higher indicates diabetes.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              2,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Fasting Plasma Glucose (FPG) Test: Measures your blood sugar after you go without food or drink (except water) for at least eight hours. A result of 126 mg/dL (7.0 mmol/L) or higher indicates diabetes.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Oral Glucose Tolerance Test (OGTT): Measures your blood sugar before and two hours after you drink a sweet liquid containing 75 grams of glucose. A blood sugar level of 200 mg/dL (11.1 mmol/L) or higher at the two-hour mark indicates diabetes .",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Random Plasma Glucose Test: Measures your blood sugar at any time of day when you have classic symptoms of high blood sugar (such as extreme thirst, frequent urination, or unexplained weight loss). A result of 200 mg/dL (11.1 mmol/L) or higher suggests diabetes."
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          1,
          5,
          6
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Confirmation Requirements"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you do not have severe, classic symptoms of high blood sugar, doctors usually require a second abnormal test result\u2014either by repeating the same test on a different day or using a second different test from the same or separate blood draw\u2014to confirm the diagnosis.",
        "reference_indexes": [
          5
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you're looking into this because of specific symptoms, would you like to share what symptoms you or someone else is experiencing or discuss common risk factors?"
      }
    ],
